WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.197
Costing the vision tier before you use it.

2
00:00:02.477 --> 00:00:05.613
Do the arithmetic before routing a corpus to a model.

3
00:00:05.893 --> 00:00:27.675
At the time of writing, Anthropic’s PDF docs estimate 1,500 to 3,000 text tokens per page, plus image tokens for each page image, with limits of 600 pages per request (100 when the request’s context window is under one million tokens) and no password-protected or encrypted files.

4
00:00:27.955 --> 00:00:38.131
Google’s docs count each PDF page as 258 tokens and allow up to 1,000 pages or 50 MB per document.

5
00:00:38.411 --> 00:00:47.435
OpenAI’s file inputs send both extracted text and page images to vision-capable models, with a 50 MB limit per request.

6
00:00:47.715 --> 00:00:52.280
These numbers change, so check the pages linked in the sources before you budget.

7
00:00:52.560 --> 00:01:03.611
A worked example: 10,000 pages at roughly 3,000 total tokens per page is 30 million input tokens, before any output.

8
00:01:03.891 --> 00:01:06.685
At the review-date list price for Claude Haiku 5.

9
00:01:06.965 --> 00:01:08.309
5 ($0.

10
00:01:08.589 --> 00:01:25.315
10 per million input tokens for prompts up to 100,000 tokens) that is about three dollars of input; on a frontier model it is tens to hundreds of dollars, and the output (the transcribed Markdown) is billed at the higher output rate.

11
00:01:25.595 --> 00:01:34.576
That is often worth paying for the 5 percent of pages a cheap parser mangles, and rarely worth paying for the 95 percent it handles fine.

12
00:01:34.856 --> 00:01:38.888
Model selection here is an evidence question, not a brand question.

13
00:01:39.168 --> 00:01:56.981
Take 30 to 50 pages that represent your corpus (include the ugly ones: tables, scans, two-column layouts), run two or three candidates (pypdf, Docling, one or two vision models), and score each page on the things your application needs: are the numbers in the tables right,

14
00:01:57.261 --> 00:01:59.864
is the reading order right, did anything get dropped?

15
00:02:00.144 --> 00:02:04.368
A half-day of this beats a month of debugging retrieval on bad text.
